description.p:
US Patent 4,742,330 discloses a capacitive flash ADC. This ADC performs 2n bit conversion using 2" parallel branches in three phases. In the first phase, offset cancellation takes place. In the second and the third phases the n-bit most significant bits (MSBs) and n-bit least significant bits (LSBs) are obtained respectively. The operation of the second phase is closely related to that of the classical flash ADC architecture with a resistive ladder which is described above. The MSBs determine the coarse range {vr.Vl+l } where the input signal lies. In the third phase, the LSBs are 

determined by dividing the coarse voltage range 

 } into 2" fine voltage levels by using 2" parallel branches, each containing log2π binary weighted capacitors. Some of these capacitors are connected to Vi and others to Vi+] to interpolate between the two extremities. The 2" comparators compare the input voltage to the generated 2" fine reference voltages in parallel.
